Ectopic Pregnancy (Ruptured)
Acute Urgent Care
Severe pelvic pain, Abnormal vaginal bleeding, Positive beta-hCG, Adnexal mass/free fluid

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-029
Reason for Visit: Sudden collapse, agonizing pelvic pain, and extreme paleness in a woman of childbearing age.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young female who suddenly collapsed in the bathroom following a brief episode of agonizing, sharp lower abdominal pain. She was pale, sweaty, and difficult to rouse. This presentation indicates acute haemorrhagic shock, highly suspicious for a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. Immediate, on-site clinical intervention was essential to manage the profound hypovolaemia and expedite rapid transport to an operating theatre, as waiting for daytime hours would be rapidly fatal.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Roommate reports] She screamed that her stomach was tearing apart, grabbed her shoulder, and then just collapsed on the floor.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a sudden onset of excruciating, unilateral left-sided pelvic pain that occurred 30 minutes ago. Prior to the collapse, the patient complained of intense left shoulder tip pain, indicating massive diaphragmatic irritation from sudden haemoperitoneum. The roommate reports the patient mentioned having abnormal, dark vaginal bleeding for the past three days and had missed her last menstrual period. The patient experienced a profound syncopal episode and is currently in a state of presyncope, unable to answer complex questions due to severe lightheadedness and confusion.
Functional Status: Lying on the bathroom floor, semi-conscious, completely unable to mobilize.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of death from exsanguination.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 75/40 | HR: 135 | Temp: 36.2 | RR: 26 | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Patient is profoundly pale, with cold, clammy skin, and is barely responsive to voice.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals profound hypotension and a rapid, thready pulse, indicating Class III/IV haemorrhagic shock. Abdominal examination reveals a distended, rigid abdomen with severe generalized tenderness and rebound guarding, consistent with massive haemoperitoneum. Bimanual pelvic exam is contraindicated in this pre-hospital setting due to obvious rupture and shock. The patient exhibits extreme pallor of the conjunctivae and nail beds. Expected investigations upon hospital arrival include a bedside transvaginal ultrasound showing massive free pelvic fluid and a complex adnexal mass, alongside a positive quantitative serum beta-hCG.
Environmental Observations: Patient is in a cramped bathroom; immediate extrication to a wider space is required for paramedic access.
Medication Reconciliation: Roommate is unaware of any medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ectopic Pregnancy (Ruptured) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ruptured Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's young age, female demographic, and presentation with vaginal bleeding and amenorrhoea, pointing firmly to an obstetric emergency.

Massive Hemorrhagic Ovarian Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to the severity of the shock and the presence of amenorrhoea, though a positive beta-hCG is the definitive test to differentiate this in the ED.

Severe Sepsis secondary to PID - Ruled out/unlikely due to the sudden, acute collapse without a preceding history of high fever, purulent discharge, or gradual deterioration.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in extremis, exhibiting signs of massive, uncontained intra-abdominal haemorrhage resulting in profound hypovolaemic shock. The clinical history of sudden severe pelvic pain, shoulder tip pain, amenorrhoea, and syncope is the textbook presentation of a catastrophically ruptured ectopic pregnancy. Urgent after-hours assessment was unequivocally life-saving; recognizing the profound shock state allowed for the immediate activation of Intensive Care Paramedics. Without this rapid escalation, the patient would have rapidly progressed to pulseless electrical activity and death.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediately positioned the patient flat with legs significantly elevated to maximize cerebral and coronary perfusion. Activated 000 for Priority 1 Intensive Care Paramedic dispatch, explicitly declaring a "hypovolaemic shock / suspected ruptured ectopic" to trigger massive transfusion protocols upon ED arrival. Patient kept strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM).
Positive Behavioral Change: (N/A in profound shock; focus is entirely on emergency stabilization and rapid extrication).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Immediate transport to a major trauma/obstetric center.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to Intensive Care Paramedics and ED Trauma Team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. Roommate instructed to clear the hallway and prop open the front door. Continuous monitoring of the patient's airway; if vomiting occurs, roll her immediately into the lateral recovery position. If breathing ceases, commence CPR.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: You have severe internal bleeding likely from a pregnancy that has burst outside the womb. This is a critical emergency requiring immediate surgery.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will immediately give you blood transfusions and take you straight to the operating room to stop the bleeding.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to move. Stay exactly as you are with your legs up. Focus on my voice and keep taking small breaths. The ambulance is almost here.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your blood pressure is critically low because of internal bleeding. We have your legs up so all your remaining blood goes to your brain and heart. The paramedics will start pushing fluids into your veins the second they arrive.
Actionable Steps: Let your roommate do all the talking. Try to stay awake by keeping your eyes open and looking at me.

